## Appendix A Definition of the \(\hat{\Gamma}_{i}\) matrices

The matrices \(\hat{\Gamma}_{1}\), \(\hat{\Gamma}_{2}\), \(\hat{\Gamma}_{1x}\), \(\hat{\Gamma}_{2x}\), \(\hat{\Gamma}_{1y}\), and \(\hat{\Gamma}_{2y}\) that appear throughout this work are defined by linearizing the functions of the three-band model around the hot spots depicted in Fig. 3. The structure of the resulting matrices can then be simplified by resorting to a representation based on Pauli matrices defined in distinct pseudospin spaces [16], which are denoted by \(\Sigma\), \(\Lambda\), and \(L\). Technically speaking, the pseudospin space \(\Sigma\) connects hot spots that can be mapped onto each other by the antiferromagnetic wavevector \(\mathbf{Q}=(\pi,\pi)\). Different pairs of hot spots connected by the wavevector \(\mathbf{Q}\) are mapped onto each other by the pseudospin space \(\Lambda\). Lastly, the pseudospin space \(L\) connects orthogonal quartet of hot spots. Following these definitions, the matrices of the three-band model can be simply written as

\[\hat{\Gamma}_{1} = -2t_{pp}\cos\delta\ \mathbb{1}_{\Sigma}\otimes\mathbb{1}_{ \Lambda}\otimes\mathbb{1}_{L}, \tag{15}\] \[\hat{\Gamma}_{2} = t_{pp}(\sin\delta\Lambda_{3}\otimes L_{3}-\Sigma_{3}\otimes \Lambda_{3})i\partial_{x}\] (16) \[- t_{pp}(\sin\delta\Lambda_{3}+\Sigma_{3}\otimes\Lambda_{3} \otimes L_{3})i\partial_{y},\] \[\hat{\Gamma}_{1x} = \gamma_{1}e^{-i\varphi\Lambda_{3}\otimes L_{3}}+\gamma_{2}e^{i \theta\Lambda_{3}\otimes L_{3}}\Sigma_{3}\otimes L_{3},\] (17) \[\hat{\Gamma}_{2x} = -\frac{1}{2}\gamma_{1}e^{-i\varphi\Lambda_{3}\otimes L_{3}}\Sigma _{3}\otimes\Lambda_{3}+\frac{1}{2}\gamma_{2}e^{i\theta\Lambda_{3}\otimes L_{3} }\Lambda_{3}\otimes L_{3},\] (18) \[\hat{\Gamma}_{1y} = \gamma_{1}e^{i\varphi\Lambda_{3}}-\gamma_{2}e^{-i\theta\Lambda_{3} }\Sigma_{3}\otimes L_{3},\] (19) \[\hat{\Gamma}_{2y} = -\frac{1}{2}\gamma_{1}e^{i\varphi\Lambda_{3}}\Sigma_{3}\otimes \Lambda_{3}\otimes L_{3}+\frac{1}{2}\gamma_{2}e^{-i\theta\Lambda_{3}}\Lambda_{3}, \tag{20}\]where \(\delta=(K_{+}-K_{-})/2\) and \(\mathbb{1}_{\Sigma}\), \(\mathbb{1}_{\Lambda}\), and \(\mathbb{1}_{L}\) are, respectively, the identity matrices in the \(\Sigma\), \(\Lambda\), and \(L\) pseudospin spaces. The parameters \(\varphi\), \(\theta\), \(\gamma_{1}\), and \(\gamma_{2}\) are defined as

\[\tan\varphi=\frac{R_{II}}{2t_{pd}}\tan\biggl{(}\frac{\delta}{2} \biggr{)}, \tag{10}\] \[\tan\theta=\frac{R_{II}}{2t_{pd}}\cot\biggl{(}\frac{\delta}{2} \biggr{)},\] (11) \[\gamma_{1}=\biggl{[}2t_{pd}^{2}\cos^{2}\biggl{(}\frac{\delta}{2} \biggr{)}+\frac{R_{II}^{2}}{2}\sin^{2}\biggl{(}\frac{\delta}{2}\biggr{)} \biggr{]}^{1/2},\] (12) \[\gamma_{2}=\biggl{[}2t_{pd}^{2}\sin^{2}\biggl{(}\frac{\delta}{2} \biggr{)}+\frac{R_{II}^{2}}{2}\cos^{2}\biggl{(}\frac{\delta}{2}\biggr{)} \biggr{]}^{1/2}. \tag{13}\]

## Appendix B Evaluation of the Matsubara sums for the mean-field equations

### Quadrupole density wave (QDW) order parameter

In order to compute the Matsubara sum in Eq. (31), we will consider that the QDW order parameter does not depend on both the frequency and the momentum. In this manner, we can rewrite this equation as

\[b(T)=\frac{3\lambda^{2}T}{16}\sum_{l,m=1}^{2}\sum_{\varepsilon_{n}}\int\frac{ D_{eff}(\varepsilon_{n},\mathbf{k})}{\mathcal{D}_{l}^{(m)}(i\varepsilon_{n}, \mathbf{k})}\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}_{l}^{(m)}(i\varepsilon_{n},\mathbf{k}) }{\partial b}\frac{d\mathbf{k}}{(2\pi)^{2}}, \tag{14}\]

where we have not written explicitly the full dependence of \(b(T)\) to not clutter up the notation.

There is a subtlety to obtain the analytic continuation of the effective bosonic propagator \(D_{eff}(\varepsilon_{n},\mathbf{k})\) since this function depends on \(|\omega|\) which is not well-defined for complex numbers. To circumvent that, we make use of the two integral formulas

\[|\omega| =-\frac{i\omega}{\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{dx}{x-i\omega}, \tag{15}\] \[\text{sgn}(\omega) =-\frac{i}{\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{dx}{x-i\omega}. \tag{16}\]

As a result, the analytic continuation of \(D_{eff}(\varepsilon_{n},\mathbf{k})\) becomes

\[D_{eff}(-iz,\mathbf{k})=\frac{1}{-i\gamma z\,\text{sgn}[\text{Im}(z)]+| \mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}}. \tag{17}\]

As may be easily concluded, \(D_{eff}(-iz,\mathbf{k})\) is not analytic in the entire complex plane. Indeed, it possesses a branch cut (see Fig. 5) which must be avoided when performing complex integration. As a result, we obtain that Eq. (14) may be rewritten as

\[b(T)\] \[=\frac{3\lambda^{2}}{16}\sum_{l,m=1}^{2}\int\frac{d\mathbf{k}}{(2 \pi)^{2}}\biggl{\{}-\frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_{\mathcal{C}_{1}}dzn_{F}(z)D_{eff}(- iz,\mathbf{k})\] \[\times\biggl{[}\frac{1}{h_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}\frac{\partial h _{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}{\partial b}+\frac{1}{\overline{h}_{l}^{(m)}(z, \mathbf{k})}\frac{\partial\overline{h}_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}{\partial b} \biggr{]}\biggr{\}}, \tag{18}\]

where we have used the result \(\mathcal{D}_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})=h_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})\overline{h}_{l} ^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})\) (see the Appendix C for details). Here \(h_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})\) and \(\frac{\partial h_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}{\partial b}\) are both polynomials with respect to \(z\) and the relation between their degrees is the following

\[\text{deg}\biggl{[}\frac{\partial h_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}{\partial b} \biggr{]}<\text{deg}[h_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})]. \tag{19}\]

As expected, a similar inequality holds for \(\overline{h}_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})\) and \(\frac{\partial\overline{h}_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}{\partial b}\). The main consequence of the result in Eq. (19) is that we can have a series expansion of the form

\[\frac{1}{h_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}\frac{\partial h_{l}^{(m)}(z, \mathbf{k})}{\partial b}+\frac{1}{\overline{h}_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}\frac{ \partial\overline{h}_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}{\partial b}\] \[=\sum_{n=1}^{N}\frac{\Delta_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})}{z-\xi_{l,n}^ {(m)}(\mathbf{k})}, \tag{20}\]

where \(N\equiv\frac{3}{4}\text{dim}(\Sigma\otimes\Lambda\otimes L\otimes\tau)\) is equal to twelve and \(\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})\) represent both the roots of \(h_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})\) (\(1\leqslant n\leqslant N/2\)) and \(\overline{h}_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})\) (\(N/2+1\leqslant n\leqslant N\)). The coefficients \(\Delta_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})\) are calculated as

\[\Delta_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})=\frac{\frac{\partial h_{l}^{(m)}(z, \mathbf{k})}{\partial b}\biggr{|}_{z=\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})}}{\frac{ \partial h_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}{\partial z}\biggr{|}_{z=\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}( \mathbf{k})}},\hskip 14.226378pt1\leqslant n\leqslant\frac{N}{2}; \tag{21}\] \[\Delta_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})=\frac{\frac{\partial\overline{h}_{l}^ {(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}{\partial b}\biggr{|}_{z=\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})}}{ \frac{\partial\overline{h}_{l}^{(m)}(z,\mathbf{k})}{\partial z}\biggr{|}_{z=\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})}},\hskip 14.226378pt\frac{N}{2}+1\leqslant n\leqslant N. \tag{22}\]

Then by substituting Eq. (20) into Eq. (18), we obtain that the mean-field equation for \(b(T)\) assumes the form

\[b(T) =\frac{3\lambda^{2}}{16}\sum_{l,m=1}^{2}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\int\Delta_{l, n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})\biggl{[}-\frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_{\mathcal{C}_{1}}dzn_{F}(z)\] \[\times\frac{D_{eff}(-iz,\mathbf{k})}{z-\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k} )}\biggr{]}\frac{d\mathbf{k}}{(2\pi)^{2}}. \tag{10}\]

The complex integral between brackets is computed by changing the integration contour from \(\mathcal{C}_{1}\) to \(\mathcal{C}_{2}\) (see Fig. 5), i.e.,

\[-\frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_{\mathcal{C}_{1}}dzn_{F}(z)\frac{D_{eff}(- iz,\mathbf{k})}{z-\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})}\] \[=-\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dxn_{F}(x)\biggl{[}\frac {1}{-i\gamma x+|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}}\frac{1}{x^{+}-\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{ k})}\] \[-\frac{1}{i\gamma x+|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}}\frac{1}{x^{-}-\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})}\biggr{]}, \tag{11}\]

where \(x^{\pm}=x\pm i\eta\) and \(\eta\to 0^{+}\). At this point, we employ the Dirac identity

\[\frac{1}{x\pm i\eta}=\mp i\pi\delta(x)+\mathcal{P}\biggl{(}\frac{1}{x}\biggr{)}, \tag{12}\]

with \(\mathcal{P}\) standing for the Cauchy principal value in order to obtain the following

\[-\frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_{\mathcal{C}_{1}}dzn_{F}(z)\frac{D_{eff}( -iz,\mathbf{k})}{z-\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})}\] \[=\frac{|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}}{[|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}]^{2}+ \gamma^{2}[\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})]^{2}}n_{F}[\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})]\] \[-\frac{\gamma}{\pi}\mathcal{P}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dx\frac{xn_{ F}(x)}{[|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}]^{2}+\gamma^{2}x^{2}}\frac{1}{x-\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}( \mathbf{k})}. \tag{13}\]

Finally after inserting the result in Eq. (B.1) into Eq. (B.1), the mean-field equation for the QDW order parameter at finite temperature can be simply expressed as

\[b(T) =\frac{3\lambda^{2}}{16}\sum_{l,m=1}^{2}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\int\biggl{\{} \frac{|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}}{[|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}]^{2}+\gamma^{2}[\xi_{l,n }^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})]^{2}}\] \[\times n_{F}[\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})]-\frac{\gamma}{\pi} \mathcal{P}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dx\frac{xn_{F}(x)}{[|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}]^{ 2}+\gamma^{2}x^{2}}\] \[\times\frac{1}{x-\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})}\biggr{\}}\Delta_{l, n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})\frac{d\mathbf{k}}{(2\pi)^{2}}. \tag{14}\]

In the limit of \(T\to 0\), the Fermi-Dirac distribution function \(n_{F}(x)\) becomes the step function \(\theta(-x)\). As a result, the integral in Eq. (B.1) involving the Cauchy principal value evaluates to

\[\lim_{T\to 0}\mathcal{P}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dx\frac{xn_{F}(x)}{[| \mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}]^{2}+\gamma^{2}x^{2}}\frac{1}{x-\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{ k})}\] \[=\frac{\pi}{2\gamma}\frac{|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}}{(|\mathbf{k}|^ {2}+m_{a})^{2}+\gamma^{2}[\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})]^{2}}\] \[+\frac{\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})}{(|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a})^{2}+ \gamma^{2}[\xi_{l,n}^{(m)}(\mathbf{k})]^{2}}\ln\biggl{[}\frac{\gamma|\xi_{l,n}^ {(m)}(\mathbf{k})|}{|\mathbf{k}|^{2}+m_{a}}\biggr{]}, \tag{15}\]

where now \(m_{a}=m_{a}(T=0)\) is the zero-temperature bosonic mass. Hence in this limit the mean-field equation

